From 7ce280be6c558ca0bbc85ca64f577e69321b8bdf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Damien Dusha <Damien Dusha>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 01:18:37 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] task_ptp: make the notification FIFO as long as the four
 FIFOs it notifies for

Every producer -- ptp_receive_enqueue(), ptp_transmit_enqueue(),
ptp_transmit_timestamp_cb(), ptp_event_enqueue() -- pushes its item onto
its own FIFO and then a notification onto sNotificationFIFO; the PTP task
takes a notification, then one item. The notification FIFO held 16
entries, the four item FIFOs 58 between them.

So a burst that leaves the task behind can fill the notification FIFO
while an item FIFO still has room. The item goes in; its notification does
not, and nothing counts or reports that. From then on the task takes every
later item from that FIFO one notification late, for good: nothing catches
it up. A received message taken late is still processed while its slot is
inside RX_TTL_MS; once traffic is slow enough that the lag is longer, the
slot has aged out by the time its turn comes, msgb_get_by_uid() returns
NULL, and the message is skipped -- every message, silently. Stranded
events are taken late in the same way, and while the event FIFO is full
of them, a new event is dropped.

Seen on a NUCLEO-H563ZI (ThreadX + NetX Duo, the CMSIS-RTOS2 binding),
after a 120 s saturation flood, the queues read over SWD:

  - as a gPTP slave of ptp4l: the PTP thread idle on an empty
    notification FIFO, with 12-13 received messages in sRxPacketFIFO and
    2-3 heartbeats in sEventFIFO. The link was carrying two or three PTP
    frames a second, so each was taken about five seconds late, past its
    2 s TTL. The board answered no Pdelay_Req; ptp4l, with no peer delay
    to it, stopped sending it Announce and Sync; the board became its own
    master and stayed so. Five times out of five. Still out of step, it
    then answered no Delay_Req as an E2E master either, and a ptp4l slave
    never reported an offset;
  - as an E2E master: 15 heartbeats in sEventFIFO (15 of 16), nothing
    else. ptp4l stayed served, but every heartbeat was now handled 15
    late, and a stop request waited behind them: unreg_task_ptp() ran
    into its timeout instead of the task's answer.

None of these recovers by itself, nor on a profile change or `ptp reset`
-- neither touches the queues -- only unreg_task_ptp() and reg_task_ptp(),
which recreate them.

The notification FIFO is now as long as the four together -- the most
items they can hold -- so a notification push cannot fail once its item
push has succeeded. That is 42 more entries; on the board, 40 B less free
heap with FreeRTOS, 132 B with ThreadX.

Host test: flexPTP's real engine, OS-less, gPTP profile, a link peer
sending Pdelay_Req. The task is starved while half a second of heartbeats
and eight of the peer's frames arrive, then runs again, and the peer goes
on sending one Pdelay_Req a second:

                                                without        with
    Pdelay_Req answered at once, with its own
    sequenceId, over the next 10 s              0/10           10/10
      (without: the first two get a stale
      request's answer, the rest none at all)
    control: no starvation                      10/10          10/10

On the board, ThreadX + NetX Duo, the same 120 s floods:

                                                without        with
    gPTP slave, with the host as master
    again 20 s after the flood                  0/5            5/5
    queues in step after the flood (SWD):
      gPTP slave                                13 rx, 2 ev    in step
      E2E master                                15 ev (twice)  in step
    `flexptp stop` after the master's flood     2.00 s, the    0.20 s
                                                timeout
    full hardware suite: S8 and 18 scenarios    5 checks fail  105/105

The FreeRTOS + lwIP firmware stayed in step under the same floods
without the change -- its binding has the same pairs; it did not happen
there -- and passes S8 and all 20 scenarios, 113 checks, with it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/flexptp/task_ptp.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/flexptp/task_ptp.c b/src/flexptp/task_ptp.c
index 3c58ede..2a14ea1 100644
--- a/src/flexptp/task_ptp.c
+++ b/src/flexptp/task_ptp.c
@@ -68,8 +68,14 @@ static osSemaphoreId_t sParked = NULL; ///< given by the PTP task once it has st
 // FIFO for incoming packets
 #if defined(FLEXPTP_NON_LINUX_OS) || defined(FLEXPTP_OSLESS)
 #define EVENT_FIFO_LENGTH (16)        ///< Event FIFO length
-#define NOTIFICATION_FIFO_LENGTH (16) ///< Notification FIFO length
 #define TX_CALLBACK_FIFO_LENGTH (10)  ///< Transmit callback FIFO length
+
+// Each item pushed onto one of the four FIFOs above is followed by one notification, and the task
+// takes one item per notification. So the notification FIFO must have room for every item they
+// can hold together: if it fills while one of them still has room, an item goes in without its
+// notification, and the task takes every later item from that FIFO one notification late, for
+// good -- a received one, once traffic is slow, past its TTL, i.e. not at all.
+#define NOTIFICATION_FIFO_LENGTH (RX_PACKET_FIFO_LENGTH + TX_PACKET_FIFO_LENGTH + EVENT_FIFO_LENGTH + TX_CALLBACK_FIFO_LENGTH) ///< Notification FIFO length
 #endif
 
 #define TX_TTL_MS (2000) ///< TTL for outbound packets
